Figure captions and native VLM descriptions:
[{"figure_index": 1, "page_no": 6, "caption": "Fig. 1  |  CellHermes  construction  by  integrating  different  self-supervised  learning techniques. a.  Comparative diagram of different foundation model construction between traditional  methods  and  our  method; In traditional methods, we usually take one of the", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nMulti-panel schematic figure labeled **a-d** describing a CellHermes-style workflow for applying/fine-tuning NLP large language models to omics and biological network data.\n\nPanel **a** compares two training strategies:\n- **Training foundation model from scratch**: single omics data are tokenized, passed into an LLM architecture, pretrained, and produce a foundation model.\n- **Fine-tuning existing NLP-LLMs (CellHermes)**: multiple omics datasets are textualized, passed into existing LLMs, and fine-tuned to produce a foundation model.\n\nPanel **b** shows emulation of self-supervised learning with question-answer pairs:\n- Source object: **gene expression data**, represented as a cell-by-gene matrix with genes labeled G1-G4.\n- Transformation: genes in cell sequences are masked using **[MASK]**.\n- Interfaces/tasks:\n  - **MLM in sequence**: prompt asks to recover masked genes from a sequence sorted by expression levels.\n  - **Autoregressive in sequence**: prompt asks to predict next gene names among highly expressed genes ordered by descent.\n- Source object: **protein-protein network**, represented as a small graph with gene/protein nodes G1-G4.\n- Transformations:\n  - **Masking node** for graph node prediction.\n  - **Masking edge** for graph link prediction.\n- Interfaces/tasks:\n  - **Mask node prediction in graph**: prompt uses masked node plus 3-hop and 2-hop neighbor lists to recover the gene.\n  - **Mask link prediction in graph**: prompt asks whether a candidate gene is connected within two hops, returning Yes/No.\n- Outputs are shown as reconstructed cells or reconstructed graphs.\n\nPanel **c** shows prompt and training design:\n- **Human-AI collaboration prompts design**: human-written prompts are expanded by AI assistance into diverse prompts.\n- **CellHermes training**: an instruction dataset containing MLM, autoregressive, mask node prediction, and mask link prediction tasks is used to instruction-tune open-source LLMs, including Llama, Qwen, and Deepseek, with LoRA, producing CellHermes.\n\nPanel **d** shows downstream model roles:\n- **LLM as an encoder**: text inputs such as “Gene BRCA1”, “Gene BRCA1 in a breast cancer cell”, and ranked gene-expression cell descriptions are mapped to gene embeddings, contextualized gene embeddings, or cell embeddings.\n- **LLM as a predictor**: CellHermes interfaces with biological databases including Perturbase, DepMap, BioGRID, CellMarker, CTD, Orphanet, and DisGeNET to predict perturbation response, cell fitness, and gene interaction.\n- **LLM as an explainer**: user provides a cell gene-expression description; CellHermes returns a natural-language explanation predicting cell state/reactivation and referencing genes/pathways visible in the schematic.\n\nVisible biological source objects include omics datasets, gene expression matrices, gene sequences, protein-protein interaction graphs, genes/proteins labeled G1-G4, BRCA1 examples, biological databases, cell states, perturbation response, cell fitness, and gene interactions."]}, {"figure_index": 2, "page_no": 10, "caption": "Fig. 2 | CellHermes as an encoder for representing genes. a. Schematic illustration of transforming  a  generative  model  into  an  embedding  model  for  representing  genes  by taking  the  last  token  embedding.  b.  ROC-AUC  of  benchmarked  models on gene-level downstream  tasks,  including  transcription  factor  range  prediction  (TF_range),  dosage", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nMulti-panel biological/computational figure about gene embedding extraction and cell-specific gene network construction using CellHermes and baseline methods.\n\nVisible panels:\n\n- **a**: Schematic workflow for **gene embedding extraction**. Input text example: `\"Gene BRCA1\"` is passed into **CellHermes (Embedding)**, producing an output vector representation.\n\n- **b**: Performance comparison table across methods and tasks. Rows include combinations such as **CellHermes+RF**, **CellHermes+XGB**, **UCE+XGB**, **LLaMA+XGB**, **GeneCompass+RF**, **scGPT+RF**, **Geneformer+XGB**, **PCA+RF**, **Random+RF**, etc. Columns include task metrics such as **TF_range**, **Dosage_sens**, **Bi_vs_Meth**, **Bi_vs_Lysine**, **GGI**, and an **Aggregate score** bar. Highest aggregate scores are around **0.85** for CellHermes-based methods.\n\n- **c**: Line plot titled **Total score**, comparing embedding input settings such as **Transcriptome-LLaMA**, **Description-only**, **Graph-only**, multiple token/embedding sizes, and **All datasets**. Two colored lines show score trends.\n\n- **d**: Violin plot titled **Correlation in GO BP**, showing Pearson correlation coefficients for methods including **CellHermes**, **LLaMA**, **GenePT**, **scGPT**, **Geneformer**, **scFoundation**, **scBERT**, **GeneCompass**, **UCE**, **PCA**, **Cell2Sentence**, and **Random**. CellHermes appears among the higher distributions.\n\n- **e**: Schematic for **cell-specific gene embedding extraction** and **cell-specific gene network construction**. Input text describes a cell with ranked gene expression, including **TMSB10, GAPH, ..., FTH1** and query gene **BRCA1**. CellHermes produces gene embeddings that are transformed into a gene network.\n\n- **f**: Line plot of **GO BP significantly enriched clusters** across cell types. Methods compared: **CellHermes**, **LLaMA**, and **Coexpression**. CellHermes and LLaMA are higher than Coexpression across most cell types.\n\n- **g**: Cell-specific gene network visualization with highlighted top differential genes. Labels include **CLEC1A**, **CLEC4D**, **CLIC2**, **STX11**, **STX7**, **TLE**, **CYTL1**, **PRCRA**, **TCEA3**, and **JAM3**. Node color indicates **node degree**.\n\n- **h**: Enrichment dot plot with enriched terms related to T cell biology and immune response, including **Positive Regulation of T Cell Activation**, **Canonical NF-kappaB Signal Transduction**, **Positive Regulation of Monocyte Differentiation**, **Hemopoiesis**, **CD4-positive Alpha-Beta T Cell Proliferation**, **B Cell Activation**, **Inflammatory Response**, **Cellular Response to Tumor Necrosis Factor**, **Lymphocyte Differentiation**, and **T Cell Differentiation**. Axes show gene ratio and adjusted p-value color scale.\n\n- **i**: Volcano plot showing differential modules. X-axis: **Log2(Fold change)**. Y-axis: **-Log10(Adjusted P-value)**. Highlighted modules include **Module_20**, **Module_15**, and **Module_11**.\n\n- **j**: Enrichment dot plot for one module with terms including **Protein Maturation**, **Protein Signaling Pathway**, **Positive Regulation of T Cell Activation**, **Lymphocyte Differentiation**, **Peptidyl-Tyrosine Phosphorylation**, **T Cell Activation**, **T Cell Receptor Signaling Pathway**, and **T Cell Differentiation**.\n\n- **k**: Enrichment dot plot for another module with immune/NK-cell-related terms including **Apoptotic Process**, **Interleukin-2-Mediated Signaling Pathway**, **Regulation of T Cell Tolerance Induction**, **Negative Regulation of T Cell Migration**, **Natural Killer Cell Activation**, **Cytokine-Mediated Signaling Pathway**, **Natural Killer Cell Mediated Cytotoxicity**, and **Natural Killer Cell Mediated Immunity**.\n\nBiological source objects visible: genes such as **BRCA1**, **TMSB10**, **GAPH**, **FTH1**, **JAM3**, **CLEC1A**, **CLEC4D**, **CLIC2**, **STX11**, **STX7**, **CYTL1**, **PRCRA**, **TCEA3**; biological processes related to **GO BP**, **T cell activation/differentiation**, **lymphocyte differentiation**, **NK cell activation**, and immune signaling.\n\nOverall finding shown: **CellHermes-derived embeddings perform strongly across benchmark gene-related tasks and produce biologically meaningful cell-specific gene networks enriched for immune and cell-type-relevant biological processes.**"]}, {"figure_index": 3, "page_no": 12, "caption": "Fig. 3 |  CellHermes as an encoder for representing cells. a. Schematic illustration of transforming a 'cell sentence' into a gene embedding with CellHermes, enabling cell type clustering and batch effect removal. This is a more concrete illustration for how we encode cell embeddings using CellHermes compared to Fig. 1d. b. UMAP visualization of different embedding  methods,  including  four  best-performing  methods  and  one  traditional  PCA method, colored by annotated batch and cell type information. c. scIB integration scores for  each  benchmarking  method  on  the  Immune  dataset,  calculated  by  the scib-metric package;  The  last three columns  indicate  the  average  scores  across  all  metrics. CellHermes-s and CellHermes-w embeddings were constructed following the protocol of", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nMulti-panel scientific figure about single-cell embedding extraction and evaluation.\n\nPanel a: Schematic workflow labeled “Cell embedding extraction.” Input is text describing a cell with genes ranked by expression, example: “TMSB10, GAPH, ..., FTH1.” The text is passed to “CellHermes (Embedding)” and converted to an output vector. The vector is then used for evaluation tasks including cell type clustering and batch integration. The evaluation cartoons indicate desirable separation by cell type and mixing by batch.\n\nPanel b: UMAP-like scatter plots comparing embedding methods. Two rows are labeled “Batch” and “Cell type.” Columns are labeled PCA, SCimilarity, scGPT, CellHermes, and CellPLM. Points are colored by batch in the top row and by cell type in the bottom row. The batch legend includes multiple datasets/samples, and the cell type legend includes immune cell populations such as CD16+ monocytes, CD4+ T cells, CD14+ monocytes, NK cells, B cells, plasma cells, erythroid progenitors, megakaryocyte progenitors, HSCs, and other progenitor/dendritic cell classes. The visual comparison shows how methods separate biological cell types while mixing or separating batches.\n\nPanel c: Quantitative benchmark table comparing methods including SCimilarity, scGPT, CellHermes-s, CellPLM, scVI, scFoundation, Nicheformer, PCA, GenePT-s, UCE, GeneCompass, CellHermes-w, scBERT, GenePT-w, and Geneformer. Metrics are grouped under “Bio conservation,” “Batch correction,” and “Aggregate score.” Bio conservation metrics include isolated labels, KMeans NMI, KMeans ARI, silhouette label, and cLISI. Batch correction metrics include silhouette batch, iLISI, KBET, and graph connectivity. Aggregate columns include batch correction, bio conservation, and total. SCimilarity has the highest visible total aggregate score around 0.60, followed closely by scGPT around 0.59 and CellHermes-s around 0.57."]}, {"figure_index": 4, "page_no": 15, "caption": "Fig. 4 | CellHermes as predictor for multi-task predictions. a. Schematic illustration of transforming different biological downstream prediction tasks into question-answer pairs. b. Composition of multi-task training instruction datasets. Task names are indicated above the horizontal lines, dataset sizes are shown below, and data sources are annotated within the corresponding pie chart segments. c. Performance of different methods on sub-task 1 (DEG prediction) within the genetic perturbation task, evaluated across three scenarios:", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nMulti-panel scientific figure about genetic interaction and perturbation prediction/classification tasks using cell/gene perturbation data and model evaluation.\n\nPanel a: Schematic comparison of two tasks. Top shows a traditional perturbation task with cells receiving a perturbation/lightning icon, converting structured biological inputs through a “Transform” step into an instruction-based interface. The prompt asks whether gene B will be affected by gene A activation-type genetic perturbation in embryonic stem cells; output is “Yes (Up).” Bottom shows a genetic interaction type classification task with gene/DNA icons and a cell, transformed into an instruction prompt asking for the genetic interaction type between two genes given Gene ENG1 in Gene EPK1; output shown as “Negative genetic interaction.”\n\nPanel b: Donut chart summarizing datasets/tasks and counts. Segments include Gene perturbation/PerturbasE with 78,488; Gene2Cell_fitness/DepMap with 26,640; Quantitative GI/BioGRID with 11,701; Gene2Disease/CTD with 4,686; Gene2Rare_disease/Orphanet with 3,019; Gene2Phenotype/DisGeNet with 2,282; Synthetic GI/BioGRID with 2,028; Phenotypic GI/BioGRID with 781; Gene2Cell_type/CellMarker with 302; and Dosage GI/BioGRID with 100.\n\nPanel c: “Perturbation evaluation scenarios” with three illustrated settings: unseen gene, gene combination, and unseen cell line. Below are heatmap-style performance tables comparing models including LLaMA, CellHermes, STAR, CellHermes instructed, and CellHermes MTL across metrics balanced accuracy, Cohen’s kappa, and weighted F1. The color scale indicates performance score from 0 to 100.\n\nPanel d: Line plots of performance versus training fraction for two subtasks. Metrics shown are balanced accuracy, Cohen’s kappa, and weighted F1. Blue line represents Subtask 1 and red line represents Subtask 2; both generally improve as training fraction increases.\n\nPanel e: “Gene to cancer cell fitness evaluation scenarios” with two settings: unseen gene and unseen cell line. Bar charts compare LLaMA, CellHermes, CellHermes (Instruct), and CellHermes (MTL) on balanced accuracy, Cohen’s kappa, and weighted F1. CellHermes variants outperform LLaMA and baseline CellHermes in the displayed metrics.\n\nPanel f: Bar charts for relation/task categories Gene2Cell_type, Gene2Disease, Gene2Phenotype, and Gene2Rare_disease. Each chart reports balanced accuracy, Cohen’s kappa, and weighted F1 for LLaMA, CellHermes, CellHermes (Instruct), and CellHermes (MTL). CellHermes instruction/MTL variants generally show higher scores than LLaMA and CellHermes baselines.\n\nVisible biological source objects include cells, genes/DNA, gene perturbation symbols, cancer cell fitness icons, and database/task sources including BioGRID, DepMap, CTD, Orphanet, DisGeNet, CellMarker, and PerturbasE. The figure presents transformation from traditional structured biological perturbation or genetic interaction inputs into natural-language instruction/output model interfaces, followed by quantitative evaluation across held-out genes, gene combinations, and cell lines."]}, {"figure_index": 5, "page_no": 18, "caption": "Fig. 5 |  CellHermes as an explainer for interpreting prediction results. a. Schematic illustration of the attention-based method for interpreting prediction results, where attention matrices from the transformer layer are extracted and sub-tokens merged into gene-level contributions. b. Schematic illustration of a text-based reasoning method for interpreting", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nMulti-panel scientific figure describing a cell phenotype prediction and reasoning framework, apparently for T cells/TCRs and gene expression.\n\nVisible panels:\n\n- **a**: “Attention-based method” schematic. A triangular attention-like matrix with labels `_BR`, `CA1`, `_E`, `N`, `O1`, `Y` is merged into a smaller matrix labeled with tokens including `CA1`, `O1`, `Y`, `BRCA`, `ENO1`. Shows aggregation/merge of attention scores.\n\n- **b**: “Text-based reasoning method” workflow. Input box contains a prompt using a ranked gene list placeholder `{Gene name list}` and a phenotype class placeholder `{phenotype class}`. A “CellHermes (Predictor)” model predicts phenotype, followed by another CellHermes reasoning interface producing an output explanation mentioning `{phenotype class}` and pathway involvement.\n\n- **c**: Splitting strategy diagrams. “Cell-level splitting” divides T cells into train and test sets. “TCR-level splitting” divides TCR groups/cell sets into train and test sets, illustrating separation by TCR identity.\n\n- **d**: Bar plots comparing “Linear model” and “CellHermes” across metrics. Two sections: cell-level splitting and TCR-level splitting. Metrics shown are balanced accuracy, Cohen’s kappa, and weighted F1. CellHermes appears higher than the linear model under cell-level splitting, while performance is closer under TCR-level splitting.\n\n- **e**: “Gene importance heatmap.” Grid of gene names colored by normalized score from 0.0 to 1.0. Genes visible include B2M, HLA-B, EEF1A1, ACTB, GAPDH, GZMA, CD8A, PGK1, TPI1, PFN1, RHOA, TRBV5-1, SH3BGRL3, ARL6IP1, CTLA4, and others. Several genes are outlined in red, indicating highlighted important genes.\n\n- **f**: Horizontal bar chart of gene counts. Genes include PFN1, ACTG1, GAPDH, CFL1, NKG7, SH3BGRL3, CCL5, TRBV9, H3F3B, and HSP90AB1. PFN1 has the highest count, near 100.\n\n- **g**: Example model interaction. Input prompt describes a T cell from metastatic melanoma patients with top highly expressed genes such as TMSB10, GAPDH, and FTH1, and asks for reasoning for a “Reactive” prediction. Output from CellHermes says the T cell is highly cytotoxic and expresses genes involved in killing, such as GZMA, GZMB, and CD247.\n\nBiological source objects: T cells, TCRs, ranked gene expression lists, phenotype classes, genes related to cytotoxicity/reactivity.\n\nTransformations/model interfaces: attention aggregation/merge, text prompt construction from gene lists and phenotype labels, CellHermes phenotype prediction, generated textual reasoning, train/test splitting by cells or TCRs, gene importance scoring/counting.\n\nFindings shown visually: CellHermes improves over a linear model in cell-level splitting metrics; highlighted genes and count bars identify frequently important genes; generated reasoning links reactive T cell prediction to cytotoxic gene expression."]}]

